  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a91c1ee7-6222-4e10-b576-4a4298d9274d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n",
    "Beyond the advantage in memory usage without any compression algorithm, the Novel Enhanced Quantum Representation can be convenient in other aspects. However, like any technique, it may also have its drawbacks, which should be carefully considered depending on the specific application."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8d61e349-3388-468d-9559-2f73445b78cf",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<h4>Filtering and Transforming</h4>\n",
    "In Classical Computation, a standard transformation can be performed with a time complexity of $O(N)$, by iterating along the full matrix. However, by taking advantage of superposition, we can potentially perform the same operation with a time complexity of $O(logN)$, achieving an exponential improvement.<br>\n",
    "But the real advantages emerge when we apply a filter. <br>\n",
    "In classical computation, applying a filter by <b>convolution</b> takes $O(KN)$, with K being the size of the filter. It is evident that with big filters, the complexity approaches $O(N^2)$. Thanks to the <b>Convolution Theorem</b>, we can reduce this complexity to $O(NlogN)$, using the <b>Fast Fourier Trasform</b>.<br>\n",
    "With Quantum Computation, we can take it a step further. The performance of a <b>Quantum Fourier Transform</b>, without considering the measurement phase, takes $O(log^2N)$, thanks to the quantum parallelism."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "60ee0ca9-2916-4d49-bd54-18415fc80768",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<h4>Pixel Access</h4>\n",
    "While every access in the Classical Computation takes $O(1)$ without affecting the other pixels, in the NEQR, informations are stored differently. Each individual access collpases the entire quantum state. Therefore, it is necessary to measure the system to get all the position-color pairs."
   ]
  },
